#{[{:db/id #edb/ref 17592186119532 :knowledge-point/_canonical-example [{:db/id #edb/ref 17592186119785 :knowledge-point/id #uuid "930466fc-c7fe-51a5-8634-818abddd0da9" :knowledge-point/title "Calculating the Principal Normal at a Given Point"}] :question/id #uuid "8cb590c5-586d-56a1-af70-d693ea055419" :question/math-academy-id "e-14996" :question/problem "The unit tangent vector to a curve at an arbitrary point is given by\n\n$$\n\\mathbf T(t) =\\sin{t} \\,\\mathbf i -\\cos{t}\\,\\mathbf j.\n$$\n\nFind the principal normal vector at the point where $t=\\dfrac{\\pi}{4}.$" :question/worked-solution "Remember that in general, the principal normal vector is given by\n\n$$\n\\mathbf N(t)= \\dfrac{\\mathbf T'(t)}{\\|\\mathbf T'(t)\\|}.\n$$\n\nFirst, we calculate $\\mathbf T'(t)$ and evaluate it at $t=\\dfrac{\\pi}{4}\\mathbin{:}$\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}{T}^{′}(t) & =\\frac{\\text{d}}{\\text{d}t}(\\operatorname{sin}⁡t\\,i-\\operatorname{cos}⁡t\\,j) \\\\  & =\\frac{\\text{d}}{\\text{d}t}(\\operatorname{sin}⁡t)\\,i-\\frac{\\text{d}}{\\text{d}t}(\\operatorname{cos}⁡t)\\,j \\\\  & =\\operatorname{cos}⁡t\\,i-(-\\operatorname{sin}⁡t)\\,j \\\\  & =\\operatorname{cos}⁡t\\,i+\\operatorname{sin}⁡t\\,j \\\\ {T}^{′}(\\frac{\\pi }{4}) & =\\operatorname{cos}⁡(\\frac{\\pi }{4})\\,i+\\operatorname{sin}⁡(\\frac{\\pi }{4})\\,j \\\\  & =\\frac{\\sqrt{2}}{2}\\,i+\\frac{\\sqrt{2}}{2}\\,j \\\\  & =\\frac{\\sqrt{2}}{2}(i+j)\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\n \n\nNext, we find the magnitude of $\\mathbf T'\\left(\\dfrac{\\pi}{4}\\right)\\mathbin{:}$\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}‖{T}^{′}(\\frac{\\pi }{4})‖ & =\\frac{\\sqrt{2}}{2}‖i+j‖ \\\\  & =\\frac{\\sqrt{2}}{2}\\sqrt{{(1)}^{2}+{(1)}^{2}} \\\\  & =\\frac{\\sqrt{2}}{2}\\sqrt{2} \\\\  & =1\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nFinally, we normalize $\\mathbf T'\\left(\\dfrac{\\pi}{4}\\right)$ and obtain the principal normal vector to the curve at the point where $t=\\dfrac{\\pi}{4}\\mathbin{:}$\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}N(\\frac{\\pi }{4}) & =\\frac{{T}^{′}(\\frac{\\pi }{4})}{‖{T}^{′}(\\frac{\\pi }{4})‖}=\\frac{\\frac{\\sqrt{2}}{2}(i+j)}{1}=\\frac{\\sqrt{2}}{2}\\,i+\\frac{\\sqrt{2}}{2}\\,j\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nTo check our result, we can verify that $\\mathbf T$ and $\\mathbf N$ are perpendicular by computing their dot product:\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}T(\\frac{\\pi }{4})\\cdot N(\\frac{\\pi }{4}) & =(\\frac{\\sqrt{2}}{2}\\,i-\\frac{\\sqrt{2}}{2}\\,j)\\cdot (\\frac{\\sqrt{2}}{2}\\,i+\\frac{\\sqrt{2}}{2}\\,j) \\\\  & =\\frac{1}{2}-\\frac{1}{2} \\\\  & =0\\,✓\\end{aligned}\n$$"}] [{:db/id #edb/ref 17592186119533 :knowledge-point/_canonical-example [{:db/id #edb/ref 17592186119786 :knowledge-point/id #uuid "8d21e1c3-1529-5471-a1e6-76f174da79c3" :knowledge-point/title "Calculating the Principal Normal at an Arbitrary Point"}] :question/id #uuid "2f92f568-0bf5-58bd-87ce-e6be8d05fe69" :question/math-academy-id "e-14997" :question/problem "Given  the curve $\\mathbf r(t)=\\left\\langle \\sqrt 3 t , \\:  \\cos t, \\: \\sin{t}\\right\\rangle,$ find the principal normal vector $\\mathbf N(t)$ at an arbitrary point on the curve." :question/worked-solution "Remember that in general, the principal normal vector is given by\n\n$$\n\\mathbf N(t)= \\dfrac{\\mathbf T'(t)}{\\|\\mathbf T'(t)\\|}.\n$$\n\nFirst, we find the unit tangent vector $\\mathbf T(t).$ To do this, we calculate $\\mathbf r'(t)$ and its magnitude as follows:\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}{r}^{′}(t) & =⟨\\frac{\\text{d}}{\\text{d}t}(\\sqrt{3}t),\\,\\frac{\\text{d}}{\\text{d}t}(\\operatorname{cos}⁡t),\\,\\frac{\\text{d}}{\\text{d}t}(\\operatorname{sin}⁡t)⟩ \\\\  & =⟨\\sqrt{3},\\,-\\operatorname{sin}⁡t,\\,\\operatorname{cos}⁡t⟩ \\\\ ‖{r}^{′}(t)‖ & =\\sqrt{(\\sqrt{3})^{2}+(-\\operatorname{sin}⁡t)^{2}+(\\operatorname{cos}⁡t)^{2}} \\\\  & =\\sqrt{3+{sin}^{2}⁡t+{cos}^{2}⁡t} \\\\  & =\\sqrt{4} \\\\  & =2\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nTherefore, the unit tangent vector is\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}T(t) & =\\frac{{r}^{′}(t)}{‖{r}^{′}(t)‖} \\\\  & =\\frac{1}{2}⟨\\sqrt{3},\\,-\\operatorname{sin}⁡t,\\,\\operatorname{cos}⁡t⟩ \\\\  & =⟨\\frac{\\sqrt{3}}{2},\\,-\\frac{\\operatorname{sin}⁡t}{2},\\,\\frac{\\operatorname{cos}⁡t}{2}⟩.\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nNext, we find the principal normal $\\mathbf{N}(t).$ To do this, we calculate $\\mathbf T'(t)$ and its magnitude as follows:\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}{T}^{′}(t) & =⟨\\frac{\\text{d}}{\\text{d}t}(\\frac{\\sqrt{3}}{2}),\\,\\frac{\\text{d}}{\\text{d}t}(-\\frac{\\operatorname{sin}⁡t}{2}),\\,\\frac{\\text{d}}{\\text{d}t}(\\frac{\\operatorname{cos}⁡t}{2})⟩ \\\\  & =⟨0,\\,-\\frac{1}{2}\\operatorname{cos}⁡t,\\,-\\frac{1}{2}\\operatorname{sin}⁡t⟩ \\\\  & =\\frac{1}{2}⟨0,\\,-\\operatorname{cos}⁡t,\\,-\\operatorname{sin}⁡t⟩ \\\\ ‖{T}^{′}(t)‖ & =\\frac{1}{2}\\sqrt{{0}^{2}+{(-\\operatorname{cos}⁡t)}^{2}+{(-\\operatorname{sin}⁡t)}^{2}} \\\\  & =\\frac{1}{2}\\sqrt{{cos}^{2}⁡t+{sin}^{2}⁡t} \\\\  & =\\frac{1}{2}\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nFinally, we normalize $\\mathbf T'(t)$ and obtain the principal normal to the curve:\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}N(t) & =\\frac{{T}^{′}(t)}{‖{T}^{′}(t)‖}=\\frac{\\frac{1}{2}⟨0,\\,-\\operatorname{cos}⁡t,\\,-\\operatorname{sin}⁡t⟩}{(\\frac{1}{2})}=⟨0,\\,-\\operatorname{cos}⁡t,\\,-\\operatorname{sin}⁡t⟩\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nTo check our result, we can verify that $\\mathbf T$ and $\\mathbf N$ are perpendicular by computing their dot product:\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}T(t)\\cdot N(t) & =⟨\\frac{\\sqrt{3}}{2},\\,-\\frac{\\operatorname{sin}⁡t}{2},\\,\\frac{\\operatorname{cos}⁡t}{2}⟩\\cdot ⟨0,\\,-\\operatorname{cos}⁡t,\\,-\\operatorname{sin}⁡t⟩ \\\\  & =\\frac{\\sqrt{3}}{2}\\cdot 0+\\frac{\\operatorname{sin}⁡t\\operatorname{cos}⁡t}{2}-\\frac{\\operatorname{cos}⁡t\\operatorname{sin}⁡t}{2} \\\\  & =0\\,✓\\end{aligned}\n$$"}] [{:db/id #edb/ref 17592186119534 :knowledge-point/_canonical-example [{:db/id #edb/ref 17592186119787 :knowledge-point/id #uuid "01fe665f-05e8-5183-9c98-cb760af0ae02" :knowledge-point/title "Calculating a Principal Normal Given Some Information About a Curve"}] :question/id #uuid "f5e6498b-73f9-5760-81bb-14e9acc495f8" :question/math-academy-id "e-14998" :question/problem "The unit tangent vector to a curve at an arbitrary point is given by\n\n$$\n\\mathbf T(t) = \\sin{t} \\cdot \\mathbf q(t).\n$$\n\nFind the principal normal vector at the point where $t = \\dfrac{\\pi}{4},$ if \n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}q(\\frac{\\pi }{4})=-\\frac{\\sqrt{2}}{2}\\,i-\\frac{\\sqrt{2}}{2}\\,j+k,\\,{q}^{′}(\\frac{\\pi }{4})=-2\\,k.\\end{aligned}\n$$" :question/worked-solution "Remember that in general, the principal normal vector is given by\n\n$$\n\\mathbf N(t)= \\dfrac{\\mathbf T'(t)}{\\|\\mathbf T'(t)\\|}.\n$$\n\nFirst, we calculate $\\mathbf T'(t)$ and evaluate it at $t=\\dfrac{\\pi}{4}\\mathbin{:}$\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}{T}^{′}(t) & =\\frac{\\text{d}}{\\text{d}t}(\\operatorname{sin}⁡t\\cdot q(t)) \\\\  & =\\frac{\\text{d}}{\\text{d}t}(\\operatorname{sin}⁡t)\\cdot q(t)+\\operatorname{sin}⁡t\\cdot \\frac{\\text{d}}{\\text{d}t}(q(t)) \\\\  & =\\operatorname{cos}⁡t\\cdot q(t)+\\operatorname{sin}⁡t\\cdot {q}^{′}(t) \\\\  \\\\ {T}^{′}(\\frac{\\pi }{4}) & =\\operatorname{cos}⁡(\\frac{\\pi }{4})\\cdot q(\\frac{\\pi }{4})+\\operatorname{sin}⁡(\\frac{\\pi }{4})\\cdot {q}^{′}(\\frac{\\pi }{4}) \\\\  & =\\frac{\\sqrt{2}}{2}(-\\frac{\\sqrt{2}}{2}\\,i-\\frac{\\sqrt{2}}{2}\\,j+k)+\\frac{\\sqrt{2}}{2}(-2\\,k) \\\\  & =-\\frac{1}{2}\\,i-\\frac{1}{2}\\,j-\\frac{\\sqrt{2}}{2}\\,k\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\n \n\nNext, we find the magnitude of $\\mathbf T'\\left(\\dfrac{\\pi}{4}\\right)\\mathbin{:}$\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}‖{T}^{′}(\\frac{\\pi }{4})‖ & =\\sqrt{{(-\\frac{1}{2})}^{2}+{(-\\frac{1}{2})}^{2}+{(-\\frac{\\sqrt{2}}{2})}^{2}} \\\\  & =\\sqrt{\\frac{1}{4}+\\frac{1}{4}+\\frac{1}{2}} \\\\  & =1\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nFinally, we normalize $\\mathbf T'\\left(\\dfrac{\\pi}{4}\\right)$ and obtain the principal normal to the curve at the point where $t=\\dfrac{\\pi}{4}\\mathbin{:}$\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}N(\\frac{\\pi }{4}) & =\\frac{{T}^{′}(\\frac{\\pi }{4})}{‖{T}^{′}(\\frac{\\pi }{4})‖} \\\\  & =\\frac{-\\frac{1}{2}\\,i-\\frac{1}{2}\\,j-\\frac{\\sqrt{2}}{2}\\,k}{1} \\\\  & =-\\frac{1}{2}\\,i-\\frac{1}{2}\\,j-\\frac{\\sqrt{2}}{2}\\,k\\end{aligned}\n$$"}] [{:db/id #edb/ref 17592186262761 :knowledge-point/_questions [{:db/id #edb/ref 17592186119785 :knowledge-point/id #uuid "930466fc-c7fe-51a5-8634-818abddd0da9" :knowledge-point/title "Calculating the Principal Normal at a Given Point"}] :question/answer-fields [{:answer-field/choices [{:answer/id #uuid "07d7c8f4-5bc4-5c4a-8d29-3a75adddbaab" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045498 :db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "\\frac{1}{\\sqrt{5}}⟨2,-1⟩" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186259947} {:answer/id #uuid "393deb48-4b73-5a3a-b922-fa3eaa3e1bb7" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045498 :db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "\\frac{1}{\\sqrt{10}}⟨-\\sqrt{3},1⟩" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186260384} {:answer/id #uuid "4f528ad2-861d-5736-bcbd-43986b22035b" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045498 :db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "\\frac{1}{\\sqrt{10}}⟨-1,-3⟩" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186260553} {:answer/id #uuid "99ef2b01-b2ae-523d-becc-16fad588d0a5" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045498 :db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "\\frac{1}{2}⟨-1,-\\sqrt{3}⟩" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186261209} {:answer/id #uuid "fff87119-33b5-5cfc-868b-95cd1174c3db" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045498 :db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "\\frac{1}{2}⟨1,\\sqrt{3}⟩" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186262120}] :answer-field/correct {:answer/id #uuid "99ef2b01-b2ae-523d-becc-16fad588d0a5" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045498 :db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "\\frac{1}{2}⟨-1,-\\sqrt{3}⟩" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186261209} :answer-field/id #uuid "afaf0fc7-b5cc-52f2-ae01-dc4ffbaccce7" :answer-field/key "selection" :answer-field/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045493 :db/ident :answer-field.type/radio} :db/id #edb/ref 17592186262438}] :question/difficulty {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045485 :db/ident :question.difficulty/easy} :question/id #uuid "0386dac5-9403-597b-be20-d67f4b02e9a9" :question/math-academy-id "q-127335" :question/problem "The unit tangent vector to a curve at an arbitrary point is given by\n\n$$\nT(t)=⟨-\\operatorname{sin}⁡2t,\\,\\operatorname{cos}⁡2t⟩.\n$$\n\nFind the principal normal vector at the point where $t=\\frac{\\pi }{6}.$" :question/worked-solution "First, we calculate ${T}^{′}(t)$ and evaluate it at $t=\\frac{\\pi }{6}:$\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}{T}^{′}(t) & =\\frac{\\text{d}}{\\text{d}t}(⟨-\\operatorname{sin}⁡2t\\,\\operatorname{cos}⁡2t⟩) \\\\  & =⟨\\frac{\\text{d}}{\\text{d}t}(-\\operatorname{sin}⁡2t),\\,\\frac{\\text{d}}{\\text{d}t}(\\operatorname{cos}⁡2t)⟩ \\\\  & =⟨-2\\operatorname{cos}⁡2t,\\,-2\\operatorname{sin}⁡2t⟩ \\\\ {T}^{′}(\\frac{\\pi }{6}) & =⟨-2\\operatorname{cos}⁡(\\frac{\\pi }{3}),\\,-2\\operatorname{sin}⁡(\\frac{\\pi }{3})⟩ \\\\  & =⟨-1,-\\sqrt{3}⟩\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\n \n\nNext, we find the magnitude of ${T}^{′}(\\frac{\\pi }{6}):$\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}‖{T}^{′}(\\frac{\\pi }{6})‖ & =‖⟨-1,-\\sqrt{3}⟩‖ \\\\  & =\\sqrt{(-1)^{2}+(\\sqrt{3})^{2}} \\\\  & =2\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nFinally, we normalize ${T}^{′}(\\frac{\\pi }{6})$ and obtain the principal normal vector to the curve at the point where $t=\\frac{\\pi }{6}:$\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}N(\\frac{\\pi }{6}) & =\\frac{{T}^{′}(\\frac{\\pi }{6})}{‖{T}^{′}(\\frac{\\pi }{6})‖} \\\\  & =\\frac{⟨-1,-\\sqrt{3}⟩}{2} \\\\  & =\\frac{1}{2}⟨-1,-\\sqrt{3}⟩\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nNote that when we find the scalar product of $N$ and $T$ at the point where $t=\\frac{\\pi }{6},$ we get\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}T(\\frac{\\pi }{6})\\cdot N(\\frac{\\pi }{6}) & =⟨-\\frac{\\sqrt{3}}{2},\\frac{1}{2}⟩\\cdot \\frac{1}{2}⟨-1,-\\sqrt{3}⟩ \\\\  & =\\frac{1}{4}((-\\sqrt{3})(-1)+(1)(-\\sqrt{3})) \\\\  & =0.\\,✓\\end{aligned}\n$$"}] [{:db/id #edb/ref 17592186262799 :knowledge-point/_questions [{:db/id #edb/ref 17592186119787 :knowledge-point/id #uuid "01fe665f-05e8-5183-9c98-cb760af0ae02" :knowledge-point/title "Calculating a Principal Normal Given Some Information About a Curve"}] :question/answer-fields [{:answer-field/choices [{:answer/id #uuid "0d17be68-2a76-543b-a622-b3654ac5d053" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045498 :db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "\\frac{1}{\\sqrt{3}}⟨-1,\\sqrt{2}⟩" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186259999} {:answer/id #uuid "6d9b5b82-03b1-5f24-9bf4-f4828136cfa0" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045498 :db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "\\frac{1}{\\sqrt{6}}⟨2,-\\sqrt{2}⟩" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186260836} {:answer/id #uuid "7a6ba1a1-0008-519d-b2c5-d78240665f7d" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045498 :db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "\\frac{1}{3}⟨-2\\sqrt{2},1⟩" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186260942} {:answer/id #uuid "bb19420d-b4ae-5cb5-8764-2269913eca36" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045498 :db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "\\frac{1}{3}⟨-1,2\\sqrt{2}⟩" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186261520} {:answer/id #uuid "e672199c-f760-5aca-81de-0a63ad98cdec" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045498 :db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "\\frac{1}{\\sqrt{6}}⟨-2,\\sqrt{2}⟩" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186261897}] :answer-field/correct {:answer/id #uuid "7a6ba1a1-0008-519d-b2c5-d78240665f7d" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045498 :db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "\\frac{1}{3}⟨-2\\sqrt{2},1⟩" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186260942} :answer-field/id #uuid "35b86093-86ba-5f1c-a960-515ef80708a9" :answer-field/key "selection" :answer-field/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045493 :db/ident :answer-field.type/radio} :db/id #edb/ref 17592186262222}] :question/difficulty {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045487 :db/ident :question.difficulty/moderate} :question/id #uuid "dc8e8967-4072-5705-809d-5e532a5002c5" :question/math-academy-id "q-127595" :question/problem "The unit tangent vector to a curve at an arbitrary point is given by\n\n$$\nT(t)=\\frac{1}{\\sqrt{1+4t}}\\cdot q(t).\n$$\n\nFind the principal normal vector at the point where $t=2,$ if \n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}q(2)=⟨1,2\\sqrt{2}⟩,\\,{q}^{′}(2)=⟨0,\\frac{\\sqrt{2}}{2}⟩.\\end{aligned}\n$$" :question/worked-solution "First, we calculate ${T}^{′}(t)$ and evaluate it at $t=2:$\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}{T}^{′}(t) & =\\frac{\\text{d}}{\\text{d}t}(\\frac{1}{\\sqrt{1+4t}}\\cdot q(t)) \\\\  & =\\frac{\\text{d}}{\\text{d}t}(\\frac{1}{\\sqrt{1+4t}})\\cdot q(t)+\\frac{1}{\\sqrt{1+4t}}\\cdot \\frac{\\text{d}}{\\text{d}t}(q(t)) \\\\  & =-\\frac{2}{(1+4t)^{3/2}}\\cdot q(t)+\\frac{1}{\\sqrt{1+4t}}\\cdot {q}^{′}(t) \\\\  \\\\ {T}^{′}(2) & =-\\frac{2}{(1+4(2))^{3/2}}\\cdot q(2)+\\frac{1}{\\sqrt{1+4(2)}}\\cdot {q}^{′}(2) \\\\  & =-\\frac{2}{27}⟨1,2\\sqrt{2}⟩+\\frac{1}{3}⟨0,\\frac{\\sqrt{2}}{2}⟩ \\\\  & =\\frac{1}{27}(-2⟨1,2\\sqrt{2}⟩+9⟨0,\\frac{\\sqrt{2}}{2}⟩) \\\\  & =\\frac{1}{27}⟨-2,\\frac{\\sqrt{2}}{2}⟩ \\\\  & =\\frac{1}{54}⟨-4,\\sqrt{2}⟩\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\n \n\nNext, we find the magnitude of ${T}^{′}(2):$\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}‖{T}^{′}(2)‖ & =\\frac{1}{54}\\sqrt{(-4)^{2}+(\\sqrt{2})^{2}} \\\\  & =\\frac{1}{54}\\cdot \\sqrt{18} \\\\  & =\\frac{\\sqrt{2}}{18}\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nFinally, we normalize ${T}^{′}(2)$ and obtain the principal normal to the curve at the point where $t=2:$\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}N(2) & =\\frac{{T}^{′}(2)}{‖{T}^{′}(2)‖} \\\\  & =\\frac{\\frac{1}{54}⟨-4,\\sqrt{2}⟩}{(\\frac{\\sqrt{2}}{18})} \\\\  & =\\frac{1}{3\\sqrt{2}}⟨-4,\\sqrt{2}⟩ \\\\  & =\\frac{1}{3}⟨-2\\sqrt{2},1⟩\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nNote that when we find the scalar product of $T$ and $N$ at the point where $t=2,$ we get\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}T(2)\\cdot N(2) & =\\frac{1}{3}q(2)\\cdot \\frac{1}{3}⟨-2\\sqrt{2},1⟩ \\\\  & =\\frac{1}{3}⟨1,2\\sqrt{2}⟩\\cdot \\frac{1}{3}⟨-2\\sqrt{2},1⟩ \\\\  & =\\frac{1}{9}(1\\cdot (-2\\sqrt{2})+2\\sqrt{2}\\cdot 1) \\\\  & =\\frac{1}{9}(-2\\sqrt{2}+2\\sqrt{2}) \\\\  & =0.\\,✓\\end{aligned}\n$$"}] [{:db/id #edb/ref 17592186120164 :knowledge-point/_questions [{:db/id #edb/ref 17592186119786 :knowledge-point/id #uuid "8d21e1c3-1529-5471-a1e6-76f174da79c3" :knowledge-point/title "Calculating the Principal Normal at an Arbitrary Point"}] :question/answer-fields [{:answer-field/choices [{:answer/id #uuid "9455b7e1-8e45-5b8d-8930-b09294afde81" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045498 :db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "-\\operatorname{sin}t" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186289026}] :answer-field/correct {:answer/id #uuid "9455b7e1-8e45-5b8d-8930-b09294afde81" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045498 :db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "-\\operatorname{sin}t" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186289026} :answer-field/id #uuid "b860cd5e-c7b3-588c-8a3f-8511ad544821" :answer-field/key "field-1" :answer-field/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045492 :db/ident :answer-field.type/blank} :db/id #edb/ref 17592186290133}] :question/difficulty {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045487 :db/ident :question.difficulty/moderate} :question/id #uuid "bbe7871a-ccc9-5a95-8370-21299423ec38" :question/math-academy-id "q-222851" :question/problem "Given  the curve $r(t)=⟨3\\operatorname{cos}⁡t,\\,3\\operatorname{sin}⁡t,\\,4t⟩$, find the principal normal vector $N(t)$ at an arbitrary point on the curve.\n\n$N(t)=⟨\\,-\\operatorname{cos}⁡t,$\n{{field-1}}$,0\\,⟩$" :question/worked-solution "First, we find the unit tangent vector $T(t).$ To do this, we calculate ${r}^{′}(t)$ and its magnitude as follows:\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}{r}^{′}(t) & =⟨\\frac{\\text{d}}{\\text{d}t}(3\\operatorname{cos}⁡t),\\,\\frac{\\text{d}}{\\text{d}t}(3\\operatorname{sin}⁡t),\\,\\frac{\\text{d}}{\\text{d}t}(4t)⟩ \\\\  & =⟨-3\\operatorname{sin}⁡t,\\,3\\operatorname{cos}⁡t,\\,4⟩ \\\\ ‖{r}^{′}(t)‖ & =\\sqrt{(-3\\operatorname{sin}⁡t)^{2}+(3\\operatorname{cos}⁡t)^{2}+(4)^{2}} \\\\  & =\\sqrt{9({sin}^{2}⁡t+{cos}^{2}⁡t)+16} \\\\  & =\\sqrt{25} \\\\  & =5\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nTherefore, the unit tangent vector is\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}T(t) & =\\frac{{r}^{′}(t)}{‖{r}^{′}(t)‖} \\\\  & =\\frac{1}{5}⟨-3\\operatorname{sin}⁡t,\\,3\\operatorname{cos}⁡t,\\,4⟩ \\\\  & =⟨-\\frac{3}{5}\\operatorname{sin}⁡t,\\,\\frac{3}{5}\\operatorname{cos}⁡t,\\,\\frac{4}{5}⟩.\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nNext, we find the principal normal $N(t).$ To do this, we calculate ${T}^{′}(t)$ and its magnitude as follows:\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}{T}^{′}(t) & =⟨\\frac{\\text{d}}{\\text{d}t}(-\\frac{3}{5}\\operatorname{sin}⁡t),\\,\\frac{\\text{d}}{\\text{d}t}(\\frac{3}{5}\\operatorname{cos}⁡t),\\,\\frac{\\text{d}}{\\text{d}t}(\\frac{4}{5})⟩ \\\\  & =⟨-\\frac{3}{5}\\operatorname{cos}⁡t,\\,-\\frac{3}{5}\\operatorname{sin}⁡t,\\,0⟩ \\\\  & =-\\frac{3}{5}⟨\\operatorname{cos}⁡t,\\,\\operatorname{sin}⁡t,\\,0⟩ \\\\ ‖{T}^{′}(t)‖ & =\\sqrt{{(-\\frac{3}{5}\\operatorname{cos}⁡t)}^{2}+{(-\\frac{3}{5}\\operatorname{sin}⁡t)}^{2}+{0}^{2}} \\\\  & =\\sqrt{\\frac{9}{25}({cos}^{2}⁡t+{sin}^{2}⁡t)} \\\\  & =\\frac{3}{5}\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nFinally, we normalize ${T}^{′}(t)$ and obtain the principal normal to the curve:\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}N(t) & =\\frac{{T}^{′}(t)}{‖{T}^{′}(t)‖}=\\frac{-\\frac{3}{5}⟨\\operatorname{cos}⁡t,\\,\\operatorname{sin}⁡t,\\,0⟩}{(\\frac{3}{5})}=⟨-\\operatorname{cos}⁡t,\\,-\\operatorname{sin}⁡t,\\,0⟩\\end{aligned}\n$$"}] [{:db/id #edb/ref 17592186290401 :knowledge-point/_questions [{:db/id #edb/ref 17592186119786 :knowledge-point/id #uuid "8d21e1c3-1529-5471-a1e6-76f174da79c3" :knowledge-point/title "Calculating the Principal Normal at an Arbitrary Point"}] :question/answer-fields [{:answer-field/choices [{:answer/id #uuid "674f313a-77f0-5fc8-abb7-91c3e1a7184e" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045498 :db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "-\\operatorname{sin}t" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186288758}] :answer-field/correct {:answer/id #uuid "674f313a-77f0-5fc8-abb7-91c3e1a7184e" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045498 :db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "-\\operatorname{sin}t" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186288758} :answer-field/id #uuid "88606f6e-77e8-504e-bf1e-6d51d3bd6834" :answer-field/key "field-1" :answer-field/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045492 :db/ident :answer-field.type/blank} :db/id #edb/ref 17592186290029}] :question/difficulty {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045487 :db/ident :question.difficulty/moderate} :question/id #uuid "a0891613-fa7a-56c3-a9d8-1b98ea108f48" :question/math-academy-id "q-223107" :question/problem "Given the curve $r(t)=\\operatorname{sin}⁡t\\,i+\\operatorname{cos}⁡t\\,j+2\\sqrt{2}\\,t\\,k$, find the principal normal vector $N(t)$ at an arbitrary point on the curve.\n\n$N(t)=$\n{{field-1}}\n$i\\,-\\operatorname{cos}⁡t$\n\n$j$" :question/worked-solution "First, we find the unit tangent vector $T(t).$ To do this, we calculate ${r}^{′}(t)$ and its magnitude as follows:\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}{r}^{′}(t) & =\\frac{\\text{d}}{\\text{d}t}(\\operatorname{sin}⁡t)\\,i+\\frac{\\text{d}}{\\text{d}t}(\\operatorname{cos}⁡t)\\,j+\\frac{\\text{d}}{\\text{d}t}(2\\sqrt{2}\\,t)\\,k \\\\  & =\\operatorname{cos}⁡t\\,i-\\operatorname{sin}⁡t\\,j+2\\sqrt{2}\\,k \\\\  \\\\ ‖{r}^{′}(t)‖ & =\\sqrt{(\\operatorname{cos}⁡t)^{2}+(-\\operatorname{sin}⁡t)^{2}+(2\\sqrt{2})^{2}} \\\\  & =\\sqrt{{cos}^{2}⁡t+{sin}^{2}⁡t+8} \\\\  & =\\sqrt{9} \\\\  & =3\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nTherefore, the unit tangent vector is\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}T(t) & =\\frac{{r}^{′}(t)}{‖{r}^{′}(t)‖} \\\\  & =\\frac{\\operatorname{cos}⁡t\\,i-\\operatorname{sin}⁡t\\,j+2\\sqrt{2}\\,k}{3} \\\\  & =\\frac{1}{3}\\operatorname{cos}⁡t\\,i-\\frac{1}{3}\\operatorname{sin}⁡t\\,j+\\frac{2}{3}\\sqrt{2}\\,k.\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nNext, we find the principal normal $N(t).$ To do this, we calculate ${T}^{′}(t)$ and its magnitude as follows:\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}{T}^{′}(t) & =\\frac{1}{3}\\frac{\\text{d}}{\\text{d}t}(\\operatorname{cos}⁡t)\\,i-\\frac{1}{3}\\frac{\\text{d}}{\\text{d}t}(\\operatorname{sin}⁡t)\\,j+\\frac{\\text{d}}{\\text{d}t}(\\frac{2}{3}\\sqrt{2})\\,k \\\\  & =-\\frac{1}{3}\\operatorname{sin}⁡t\\,i-\\frac{1}{3}\\operatorname{cos}⁡t\\,j+0\\,k \\\\  & =-\\frac{1}{3}\\operatorname{sin}⁡t\\,i-\\frac{1}{3}\\operatorname{cos}⁡t\\,j \\\\  \\\\ ‖{T}^{′}(t)‖ & =\\sqrt{{(-\\frac{1}{3}\\operatorname{sin}⁡t)}^{2}+{(-\\frac{1}{3}\\operatorname{cos}⁡t)}^{2}+{0}^{2}} \\\\  & =\\sqrt{\\frac{1}{9}({sin}^{2}⁡t+{cos}^{2}⁡t)} \\\\  & =\\frac{1}{3}\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nFinally, we normalize ${T}^{′}(t)$ and obtain the principal normal to the curve:\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}N(t) & =\\frac{{T}^{′}(t)}{‖{T}^{′}(t)‖} \\\\  & =\\frac{-\\frac{1}{3}\\operatorname{sin}⁡t\\,i-\\frac{1}{3}\\operatorname{cos}⁡t\\,j}{(\\frac{1}{3})} \\\\  & =-\\operatorname{sin}⁡t\\,i-\\operatorname{cos}⁡t\\,j\\end{aligned}\n$$"}] [{:db/id #edb/ref 17592186120242 :knowledge-point/_questions [{:db/id #edb/ref 17592186119785 :knowledge-point/id #uuid "930466fc-c7fe-51a5-8634-818abddd0da9" :knowledge-point/title "Calculating the Principal Normal at a Given Point"}] :question/answer-fields [{:answer-field/choices [{:answer/id #uuid "a874d8e3-9a22-519e-9812-7190479ca678" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045498 :db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "\\frac{3}{5}i - \\frac{4}{5}k" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186117612} {:answer/id #uuid "c84f8d58-bc46-54b6-a742-c27b8da8ade3" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045498 :db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "-\\frac{3}{5}j - \\frac{4}{5}k" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186117613} {:answer/id #uuid "e2170b0a-e694-5929-96ce-1fe3794d1111" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045498 :db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "\\frac{4}{5}j - \\frac{3}{5}k" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186117614} {:answer/id #uuid "52575909-820a-5276-b2d2-7400ea1affec" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045498 :db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "\\frac{3}{5}i + \\frac{4}{5}j" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186117615} {:answer/id #uuid "5bb4c81a-b3c7-5508-91d2-d81d952a3208" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045498 :db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "-\\frac{4}{5}i - \\frac{3}{5}j" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186117616}] :answer-field/id #uuid "a053211b-e5f5-5c7e-af98-df1f8ec16472" :answer-field/key "selection" :answer-field/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045493 :db/ident :answer-field.type/radio} :db/id #edb/ref 17592186117611} {:answer-field/choices [{:answer/id #uuid "1b3ad161-64a2-5b71-ad24-c120e8ec6285" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045498 :db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "\\frac{3}{5}\\,i-\\frac{4}{5}\\,k" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186398741} {:answer/id #uuid "9b7e3c75-5535-5316-bea0-bf6c928449b0" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045498 :db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "\\frac{4}{5}\\,j-\\frac{3}{5}\\,k" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186398742} {:answer/id #uuid "6a162c78-4b2f-5cb3-bbfc-9d6ac2a6eb14" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045498 :db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "\\frac{3}{5}\\,i+\\frac{4}{5}\\,j" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186398743} {:answer/id #uuid "d2f7e9c3-d0a4-5c14-ad36-84ce7ce201f9" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045498 :db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "-\\frac{4}{5}\\,i-\\frac{3}{5}\\,j" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186398744} {:answer/id #uuid "cbf33e14-d217-5957-83e1-d43d4854445a" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045498 :db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "-\\frac{3}{5}\\,j-\\frac{4}{5}\\,k" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186398745}] :answer-field/correct {:answer/id #uuid "cbf33e14-d217-5957-83e1-d43d4854445a" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045498 :db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "-\\frac{3}{5}\\,j-\\frac{4}{5}\\,k" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186398745} :answer-field/id #uuid "2ffbf219-9fc7-55d4-a011-546b8183375e" :answer-field/key "selection" :answer-field/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045493 :db/ident :answer-field.type/radio} :db/id #edb/ref 17592186398740}] :question/difficulty {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045487 :db/ident :question.difficulty/moderate} :question/id #uuid "fee02912-87a3-5a99-92ba-f8ae9f270c88" :question/math-academy-id "q-33233" :question/problem "The equations of a curve $r(t)$ and its corresponding unit tangent vector $T(t)$ at an arbitrary point are given below. Find the principal normal vector to that curve at the point $P(0,3,4).$\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}r(t) & =5\\operatorname{cos}⁡t\\,i+3\\operatorname{sin}⁡t\\,j+4\\operatorname{sin}⁡t\\,k,\\,t\\in [0,2\\pi ) \\\\ T(t) & =-\\operatorname{sin}⁡t\\,i+\\frac{3}{5}\\operatorname{cos}⁡t\\,j+\\frac{4}{5}\\operatorname{cos}⁡t\\,k,\\,t\\in [0,2\\pi )\\end{aligned}\n$$" :question/worked-solution "First, we find the value of $t$ that corresponds to the point $P(0,3,4)$ on the given curve. We know that:\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}r(t) & =⟨0,3,4⟩ \\\\ 5\\operatorname{cos}⁡t\\,i+3\\operatorname{sin}⁡t\\,j+4\\operatorname{sin}⁡t\\,k & =0\\text{ }i+3\\text{ }j+4\\text{ }k\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nEquating the components of $i$, $j$, and $k$ gives:\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}{\\begin{aligned}5\\operatorname{cos}⁡t=0 \\\\ 3\\operatorname{sin}⁡t=3 \\\\ 4\\operatorname{sin}⁡t=4\\end{aligned}\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nFrom the first equality, $t=\\frac{\\pi }{2}.$ This value also satisfies the other two equations. Therefore, $P(0,3,4)$ is at the tip of the vector $r(\\frac{\\pi }{2}).$ \n\nNow, we calculate ${T}^{′}(t)$ and evaluate it at $t=\\frac{\\pi }{2}:$\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}{T}^{′}(t) & =\\frac{\\text{d}}{\\text{d}t}(-\\operatorname{sin}⁡t)\\,i+\\frac{\\text{d}}{\\text{d}t}(\\frac{3}{5}\\operatorname{cos}⁡t)\\,j+\\frac{\\text{d}}{\\text{d}t}(\\frac{4}{5}\\operatorname{cos}⁡t)\\,k \\\\  & =-\\operatorname{cos}⁡t\\,i-\\frac{3}{5}\\operatorname{sin}⁡t\\,j-\\frac{4}{5}\\operatorname{sin}⁡t\\,k \\\\ {T}^{′}(\\frac{\\pi }{2}) & =-\\operatorname{cos}⁡\\frac{\\pi }{2}\\,i-\\frac{3}{5}\\operatorname{sin}⁡\\frac{\\pi }{2}\\,j-\\frac{4}{5}\\operatorname{sin}⁡\\frac{\\pi }{2}\\,k \\\\  & =-\\frac{3}{5}\\,j-\\frac{4}{5}\\,k\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nNext, we find the magnitude of ${T}^{′}(\\frac{\\pi }{2}):$\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}‖{T}^{′}(\\frac{\\pi }{2})‖ & =\\sqrt{(0)^{2}+{(-\\frac{3}{5})}^{2}+{(-\\frac{4}{5})}^{2}} \\\\  & =\\sqrt{\\frac{9}{25}+\\frac{16}{25}} \\\\  & =1\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nFinally, we normalize ${T}^{′}(\\frac{\\pi }{2})$ and obtain the principal normal to the curve $r(t)$ at the point $P(0,3,4):$\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}N(t) & =\\frac{{T}^{′}(\\frac{\\pi }{2})}{‖{T}^{′}(\\frac{\\pi }{2})‖}=\\frac{-\\frac{3}{5}\\,j-\\frac{4}{5}\\,k}{1}=-\\frac{3}{5}\\,j-\\frac{4}{5}\\,k\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nNote that when we find the scalar product of $T$ and $N$ at the point where at $t=\\frac{\\pi }{2},$ we get\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}T(\\frac{\\pi }{2})\\cdot N(\\frac{\\pi }{2}) & =(-i)\\cdot (-\\frac{3}{5}\\,j-\\frac{4}{5}\\,k) \\\\  & =\\frac{3}{5}(i\\cdot j)+\\frac{4}{5}(i\\cdot k) \\\\  & =0.\\,✓\\end{aligned}\n$$"}] [{:db/id #edb/ref 17592186120252 :knowledge-point/_questions [{:db/id #edb/ref 17592186119785 :knowledge-point/id #uuid "930466fc-c7fe-51a5-8634-818abddd0da9" :knowledge-point/title "Calculating the Principal Normal at a Given Point"}] :question/answer-fields [{:answer-field/choices [{:answer/id #uuid "a92a28b8-07dc-5cf4-a54e-b70947b5b250" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045498 :db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "⟨ - \\frac{2}{3}, \\frac{2}{3},-\\frac{1}{3}⟩" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186117672} {:answer/id #uuid "bc03cc21-974e-53e4-bfa8-93567baa629d" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045498 :db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "⟨\\frac{2}{3},-\\frac{2}{3}, \\frac{1}{3}⟩" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186117673} {:answer/id #uuid "2e221a65-a44e-5346-8777-5df15d203193" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045498 :db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "⟨\\frac{2}{3},-\\frac{1}{3},-\\frac{2}{3}⟩" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186117674} {:answer/id #uuid "60003406-0ebf-50a1-a52d-29c5034c9ea6" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045498 :db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "⟨\\frac{2}{3},-\\frac{1}{3}, \\frac{2}{3}⟩" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186117675} {:answer/id #uuid "7647f526-5629-558c-be6f-da2cae5bb2e3" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045498 :db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "⟨\\frac{2}{3}, \\frac{1}{3}, \\frac{2}{3}⟩" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186117676}] :answer-field/id #uuid "4aee8c82-8119-5217-bc70-4d42d2a818f7" :answer-field/key "selection" :answer-field/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045493 :db/ident :answer-field.type/radio} :db/id #edb/ref 17592186117671}] :question/id #uuid "2f555a45-5f75-599a-8fee-db06b9f6384d" :question/math-academy-id "q-33493" :question/problem "The unit tangent vector to a curve at an arbitrary point is given by\n$T(t) = \\frac{1}{t^{2} + 2}⟨ - 2, 2t, t^{2}⟩$.\nFind the principal normal vector at the point where $t = 2$."}] [{:db/id #edb/ref 17592186323725 :knowledge-point/_questions [{:db/id #edb/ref 17592186119786 :knowledge-point/id #uuid "8d21e1c3-1529-5471-a1e6-76f174da79c3" :knowledge-point/title "Calculating the Principal Normal at an Arbitrary Point"}] :question/answer-fields [{:answer-field/choices [{:answer/id #uuid "5871484e-21bb-5092-b09c-e08bf26d1e6d" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045498 :db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "\\operatorname{sin}t" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186321842}] :answer-field/correct {:answer/id #uuid "5871484e-21bb-5092-b09c-e08bf26d1e6d" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045498 :db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "\\operatorname{sin}t" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186321842} :answer-field/id #uuid "f9dd7521-e5a8-502d-93ab-04dd55f565ed" :answer-field/key "field-1" :answer-field/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045492 :db/ident :answer-field.type/blank} :db/id #edb/ref 17592186323659}] :question/difficulty {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045487 :db/ident :question.difficulty/moderate} :question/id #uuid "e77a13f7-0f57-5d23-af8b-ebbc48aa1422" :question/math-academy-id "q-358783" :question/problem "Given the curve $r(t)=⟨3\\operatorname{cos}⁡t,\\,-3\\operatorname{sin}⁡t,\\,4t⟩$, find the principal normal vector $N(t)$ at an arbitrary point on the curve.\n\n$N(t)=⟨-\\operatorname{cos}⁡t,$\n{{field-1}}$,0\\,⟩$" :question/worked-solution "First, we find the unit tangent vector $T(t).$ To do this, we calculate ${r}^{′}(t)$ and its magnitude as follows:\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}{r}^{′}(t) & =⟨\\frac{\\text{d}}{\\text{d}t}(3\\operatorname{cos}⁡t),\\,\\frac{\\text{d}}{\\text{d}t}(-3\\operatorname{sin}⁡t),\\,\\frac{\\text{d}}{\\text{d}t}(4t)⟩ \\\\  & =⟨-3\\operatorname{sin}⁡t,\\,-3\\operatorname{cos}⁡t,\\,4⟩ \\\\ ‖{r}^{′}(t)‖ & =\\sqrt{(-3\\operatorname{sin}⁡t)^{2}+(-3\\operatorname{cos}⁡t)^{2}+(4)^{2}} \\\\  & =\\sqrt{9({sin}^{2}⁡t+{cos}^{2}⁡t)+16} \\\\  & =\\sqrt{25} \\\\  & =5\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nTherefore, the unit tangent vector is\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}T(t) & =\\frac{{r}^{′}(t)}{‖{r}^{′}(t)‖} \\\\  & =\\frac{1}{5}⟨-3\\operatorname{sin}⁡t,\\,-3\\operatorname{cos}⁡t,\\,4⟩ \\\\  & =⟨-\\frac{3}{5}\\operatorname{sin}⁡t,\\,-\\frac{3}{5}\\operatorname{cos}⁡t,\\,\\frac{4}{5}⟩.\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nNext, we find the principal normal $N(t).$ To do this, we calculate ${T}^{′}(t)$ and its magnitude as follows:\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}{T}^{′}(t) & =⟨\\frac{\\text{d}}{\\text{d}t}(-\\frac{3}{5}\\operatorname{sin}⁡t),\\,\\frac{\\text{d}}{\\text{d}t}(-\\frac{3}{5}\\operatorname{cos}⁡t),\\,\\frac{\\text{d}}{\\text{d}t}(\\frac{4}{5})⟩ \\\\  & =⟨-\\frac{3}{5}\\operatorname{cos}⁡t,\\,\\frac{3}{5}\\operatorname{sin}⁡t,\\,0⟩ \\\\  & =\\frac{3}{5}⟨-\\operatorname{cos}⁡t,\\,\\operatorname{sin}⁡t,\\,0⟩ \\\\ ‖{T}^{′}(t)‖ & =\\sqrt{{(-\\frac{3}{5}\\operatorname{cos}⁡t)}^{2}+{(\\frac{3}{5}\\operatorname{sin}⁡t)}^{2}+{0}^{2}} \\\\  & =\\sqrt{\\frac{9}{25}({cos}^{2}⁡t+{sin}^{2}⁡t)} \\\\  & =\\frac{3}{5}\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nFinally, we normalize ${T}^{′}(t)$ and obtain the principal normal to the curve:\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}N(t) & =\\frac{{T}^{′}(t)}{‖{T}^{′}(t)‖}=\\frac{\\frac{3}{5}⟨-\\operatorname{cos}⁡t,\\,\\operatorname{sin}⁡t,\\,0⟩}{(\\frac{3}{5})}=⟨-\\operatorname{cos}⁡t,\\,\\operatorname{sin}⁡t,\\,0⟩\\end{aligned}\n$$"}] [{:db/id #edb/ref 17592186120520 :knowledge-point/_questions [{:db/id #edb/ref 17592186119787 :knowledge-point/id #uuid "01fe665f-05e8-5183-9c98-cb760af0ae02" :knowledge-point/title "Calculating a Principal Normal Given Some Information About a Curve"}] :question/answer-fields [{:answer-field/choices [{:answer/id #uuid "826649c3-ffe7-5f54-9966-eebc83615bcf" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045498 :db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "-j" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186119235} {:answer/id #uuid "8616fccd-bcb5-58e3-ba7b-e4f994cec92c" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045498 :db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "-\\frac{\\sqrt{2}}{2}i + \\frac{\\sqrt{2}}{2}j" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186119236} {:answer/id #uuid "af7b3031-a20c-5778-a8d4-9072e940f52f" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045498 :db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "\\frac{\\sqrt{5}}{5}i - \\frac{2\\sqrt{5}}{5}j" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186119237} {:answer/id #uuid "f695d4cc-4d27-5342-b366-23348b8d74b4" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045498 :db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "\\frac{\\sqrt{2}}{2}i + \\frac{\\sqrt{2}}{2}j" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186119238} {:answer/id #uuid "030380d0-84f6-5258-88d6-58f0ec8a168f" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045498 :db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "i + j" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186119239}] :answer-field/id #uuid "74e24ea7-f7a1-5490-a29b-1f9c2dc00269" :answer-field/key "selection" :answer-field/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045493 :db/ident :answer-field.type/radio} :db/id #edb/ref 17592186119234}] :question/id #uuid "7ee0cbfa-763d-5927-b2fe-7883ea79f8f3" :question/math-academy-id "q-74864" :question/problem "The unit tangent vector to a curve at an arbitrary point is given by\n$T(t) = \\sin t \\cdot q(t)$.\n\nFind the principal normal vector at the point where $t = \\frac{π}{2}$, if\n$q(\\frac{π}{2}) = i, q^{′}(\\frac{π}{2}) =-j$."}]}
